::ALG1_U05_L05_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(x^2-9=0\). {
=A. \(x=-3\) and \(x=3\)#Correct. This is a difference of squares: \((x-3)(x+3)=0\).
~B. \(x=9\) only#Squaring a number to get 9 gives two real solutions, not one solution of 9.
~C. \(x=-9\) and \(x=9\)#These values are the constant's opposites, not the square roots of 9.
~D. \(x=0\) and \(x=9\)#Substituting 0 does not make \(x^2-9\) equal 0.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A quadratic height model has zeros at \(t=0\) and \(t=5\). What does \(t=5\) usually represent? {
~A. The starting height#The starting height is the y-intercept at \(t=0\), not the later zero.
~B. The maximum height#The maximum height occurs at the vertex, not at a zero.
=C. The time the object returns to the ground#Correct. A zero in a height model means height is 0.
~D. The axis of symmetry#The axis of symmetry is a vertical line, not the later zero.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(x^2+x-12=0\). {
~A. \(x=3\) and \(x=4\)#These values do not create opposite signs needed for \(-12\).
=B. \(x=-4\) and \(x=3\)#Correct. The equation factors as \((x+4)(x-3)=0\).
~C. \(x=4\) and \(x=-3\)#These values would give factors \((x-4)(x+3)\), producing \(x^2-x-12\).
~D. \(x=-3\) and \(x=-4\)#Both negative roots would produce a positive middle coefficient of 7.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \((x-8)(x+2)=0\). {
~A. \(x=-8\) and \(x=2\)#The signs are reversed from the factors.
~B. \(x=8\) and \(x=2\)#The factor \(x+2\) gives \(-2\), not 2.
~C. \(x=-8\) and \(x=-2\)#The factor \(x-8\) gives 8, not \(-8\).
=D. \(x=8\) and \(x=-2\)#Correct. Set each factor equal to zero.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A garden has side lengths \(x\) and \(x+5\) and area 84. Which equation models the area? {
=A. \(x(x+5)=84\)#Correct. Area is the product of the side lengths.
~B. \(2x+10=84\)#This uses a perimeter-style expression, not area.
~C. \(x+5=84\)#This uses only one side length.
~D. \(x^2+25=84\)#This squares each part separately and misses the \(5x\) term.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q06::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q06] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(x^2-2x-15=0\). {
~A. \(x=3\) and \(x=-5\)#These roots would produce \(x^2+2x-15\), not the given equation.
=B. \(x=-3\) and \(x=5\)#Correct. The equation factors as \((x-5)(x+3)=0\).
~C. \(x=3\) and \(x=5\)#Both positive roots would produce a negative constant term only if the factors had opposite signs.
~D. \(x=-3\) and \(x=-5\)#Both negative roots would produce a positive middle term.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q07::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q07] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A model gives solutions \(x=-4\) and \(x=6\) for a side length. Which solution is reasonable? {
~A. \(-4\), because it is smaller#A side length cannot be negative in this context.
~B. Both, because every algebraic solution is always reasonable#Context can rule out solutions that do not make sense.
~C. Neither, because quadratics cannot model side lengths#Quadratic equations can model area and side length relationships.
=D. \(6\), because side length must be positive#Correct. Negative length is not meaningful, so 6 is the reasonable solution.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q08::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q08] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] What are the zeros of \(y=(x+6)(x-1)\)? {
~A. \(6\) and \(-1\)#These reverse the signs from the factors.
~B. \(6\) and \(1\)#The factor \(x+6\) gives \(-6\), not 6.
~C. \(-6\) and \(-1\)#The factor \(x-1\) gives 1, not \(-1\).
=D. \(-6\) and \(1\)#Correct. Set each factor equal to 0.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q09::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q09] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Which factorization helps solve \(x^2+7x+10=0\)? {
=A. \((x+5)(x+2)=0\)#Correct. The factors multiply to 10 and add to 7.
~B. \((x-5)(x-2)=0\)#This gives \(x^2-7x+10\), not the original equation.
~C. \((x+10)(x+1)=0\)#These factors multiply to 10 but add to 11.
~D. \((x-10)(x-1)=0\)#These factors produce the wrong signs and middle term.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q10::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q10] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A quadratic profit model has zeros at 20 and 80 items sold. What do these zeros represent? {
~A. The item prices#Zeros are input values where profit is 0, not prices.
~B. The maximum profit#The maximum profit occurs at the vertex.
~C. The fixed cost#The fixed cost is not shown directly by both zeros.
=D. Break-even sales amounts#Correct. Profit is 0 at break-even points.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q11::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q11] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(x^2-10x+25=0\). {
~A. \(x=-5\) only#The factor is \(x-5\), not \(x+5\).
=B. \(x=5\) only#Correct. The equation is \((x-5)^2=0\), so the repeated solution is \(x=5\).
~C. \(x=-5\) and \(x=5\)#This would match \(x^2-25=0\), not the given equation.
~D. \(x=25\) only#The solution is not the constant term.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q12::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q12] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Which equation has solutions \(x=-2\) and \(x=7\)? {
~A. \((x-2)(x+7)=0\)#This gives roots 2 and \(-7\).
~B. \((x+2)(x+7)=0\)#This gives roots \(-2\) and \(-7\).
=C. \((x+2)(x-7)=0\)#Correct. The factors produce roots \(-2\) and 7.
~D. \((x-2)(x-7)=0\)#This gives roots 2 and 7.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q13::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q13] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A ball is launched from the ground and lands after 4 seconds. Which pair of zeros fits the context? {
=A. \(0\) and \(4\)#Correct. Ground level at launch and landing means height is 0 at those times.
~B. \(-4\) and \(0\)#A negative time does not describe the landing after launch.
~C. \(2\) and \(4\)#This misses the launch time at 0.
~D. \(4\) and \(8\)#This does not include the ground-level launch time.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q14::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q14] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(2x^2-8x=0\). {
~A. \(x=2\) and \(x=8\)#These values come from parts of the coefficients, not from factoring.
~B. \(x=-4\) and \(x=0\)#The factor \(x-4\) gives \(x=4\), not \(-4\).
=C. \(x=0\) and \(x=4\)#Correct. Factor \(2x(x-4)=0\).
~D. \(x=8\) only#There are two factors that can be zero.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q15::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q15] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] The solutions to an area equation are \(x=-9\) and \(x=4\). What should be done with \(-9\)? {
~A. Keep it because it is farther from zero#Distance from zero does not make a negative length meaningful.
~B. Average it with 4#Averaging solutions does not interpret the area context.
~C. Change it to positive 9#Changing the sign creates a value that was not a solution.
=D. Reject it because a length cannot be negative#Correct. Negative side length is not meaningful.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q16::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q16] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(x^2+4x=0\). {
~A. \(x=4\) only#This misses the factor \(x=0\) and reverses the sign of the other solution.
=B. \(x=0\) and \(x=-4\)#Correct. Factor \(x(x+4)=0\).
~C. \(x=-4\) only#This misses the solution from the factor \(x=0\).
~D. \(x=0\) and \(x=4\)#The factor \(x+4\) gives \(-4\), not 4.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q17::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q17] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Which step should come first when solving \(x^2-6x+8=0\) by factoring? {
=A. Rewrite it as \((x-2)(x-4)=0\).#Correct. Factoring prepares the equation for the zero-product property.
~B. Divide every term by x.#Dividing by x can lose a solution and does not factor the trinomial.
~C. Set \(x^2=8\).#This ignores the middle term.
~D. Add the roots before factoring.#The roots are not known until after solving.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q18::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q18] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] What do the x-intercepts of a quadratic graph represent for the equation \(f(x)=0\)? {
~A. The maximum or minimum output#The vertex gives the maximum or minimum.
=B. The solutions to the equation#Correct. X-intercepts occur where the output is 0.
~C. The y-intercept only#The y-intercept occurs where \(x=0\), not where \(f(x)=0\) in general.
~D. The axis of symmetry#The axis of symmetry is a vertical line through the vertex.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q19::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q19] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(x^2-11x+30=0\). {
~A. \(x=-5\) and \(x=-6\)#Both negative roots would produce a positive middle term.
~B. \(x=3\) and \(x=10\)#These multiply to 30 but add to 13.
=C. \(x=5\) and \(x=6\)#Correct. \((x-5)(x-6)=0\).
~D. \(x=-3\) and \(x=-10\)#These roots produce the wrong middle term.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q20::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q20] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A rectangle has area 40 and side lengths \(x\) and \(x+6\). Which positive solution is reasonable if the equation gives \(x=-10\) and \(x=4\)? {
~A. \(-10\)#Negative side length is not reasonable.
~B. Both solutions#Only values that fit the context should be kept.
=C. \(4\)#Correct. A side length can be 4, while \(-10\) is not meaningful.
~D. Neither solution#The positive solution can represent a side length.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q21::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q21] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(x^2+9x+20=0\). {
=A. \(x=-4\) and \(x=-5\)#Correct. The equation factors as \((x+4)(x+5)=0\).
~B. \(x=4\) and \(x=5\)#The roots must be negative because both factors are positive in \((x+4)(x+5)\).
~C. \(x=-2\) and \(x=-10\)#These multiply to 20 but add to \(-12\), not \(-9\) as roots.
~D. \(x=2\) and \(x=10\)#These values do not produce the given middle term.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q22::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q22] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A quadratic model has zeros at \(-1\) and \(9\). In a time context, which zero is usually meaningful after the start? {
~A. \(-1\)#Negative elapsed time usually does not make sense in a real-time context.
~B. Both equally#Context determines whether a solution is reasonable.
=C. \(9\)#Correct. Negative time is usually outside the context, while 9 can represent elapsed time.
~D. Neither, because zeros cannot be times#Zeros can represent times when height or another output equals 0.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q23::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q23] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] What is the solution set for \(x(x-11)=0\)? {
~A. \(x=11\) only#This misses the zero from the factor \(x\).
=B. \(x=0\) and \(x=11\)#Correct. Each factor can equal 0.
~C. \(x=-11\) and \(x=0\)#The factor \(x-11\) gives 11, not \(-11\).
~D. \(x=0\) only#This misses the solution from \(x-11=0\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q24::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q24] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Which equation is already in a form ready for the zero-product property? {
~A. \(x^2+5x+6=0\)#This still needs to be factored.
~B. \(x^2=25\)#This can be solved, but it is not written as a product equal to zero.
~C. \(x^2-3x=10\)#This is not set equal to zero or factored.
=D. \((x-1)(x+4)=0\)#Correct. It is a product of factors equal to zero.




}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q25::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q25] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] Why is checking context important after solving a quadratic model? {
=A. Some algebraic solutions may not make sense for quantities such as time or length.#Correct. Context determines which solutions are reasonable.
~B. Quadratic equations never have real-world solutions.#Quadratics often model real contexts.
~C. Factoring always creates extra false solutions.#Factoring correctly preserves solutions, but context may still rule some out.
~D. Context changes the arithmetic of the equation.#The equation's arithmetic stays the same; context affects interpretation.




}


